WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.280
Let u be odd and greater than
one. The normalized polynomials c,

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.680
d, and g each have product with
their star equal to u squared.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.480
At every subset input used below,
each evaluated value has magnitude u.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.160
In particular, none is zero.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.940
Let I be the distinct primes dividing u.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.640
Choose a primitive p-th root
zeta sub p for each prime.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.260
For a subset S, multiply its
chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S;

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.900
the empty subset gives one.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.020
Delta of f is the alternating
product of these evaluations.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.600
Even subsets contribute above the
fraction bar, and odd subsets below.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:47.020
Node four proves that each of the
three Delta values is a root of

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.680
unity of odd order. We use
that theorem as an input.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:57.020
Use Delta of c as the reference. Define
R as Delta of d divided by Delta of c,

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.060
and W as Delta of g divided by Delta of c.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.600
These are well-defined complex
quotients of Delta values.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.640
They do not compare individual
polynomial coefficients.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.940
A common odd exponent
will control their orders.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.190
Suppose alpha to the m equals
one and beta to the n equals one,

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.753
with m and n positive and odd. Raise
alpha divided by beta to the product m n.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:26.540
Regrouping the powers gives alpha to the
m, raised to n, divided by beta to the n,

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.870
raised to m. Both numerator
and denominator become one.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.300
The quotient therefore has order
dividing the odd number m n.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.880
Its order is odd as well.
This applies to both R and W.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.180
Order one is included.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.730
For the guiding case u equals three,

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.230
set omega equal to the exponential
of two pi i over three.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.880
The two subset roots are one and omega,

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.200
so Delta of f equals f
of one over f of omega.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.580
Unpacking R gives d of one over c of one,
divided by d of omega over c of omega.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.260
W is the same ratio of
ratios with g in place of d.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.080
The ratio at input one is on
top; the ratio at omega is below.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:16.880
In this case, Node four restricts
the three Delta values to one, omega,

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.100
and omega squared.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.880
Dividing these cubic roots stays
in the same three-element set.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:28.080
Thus each comparison has those three
candidates. Keep all three available.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:34.080
These points represent complex phases,
not residues modulo one plus i.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.480
If R equals one, the relative ratio d over
c is unchanged between the two inputs.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.760
The analogous statement
holds for W and g over c.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.740
This does not say that those two
relative ratios equal each other,

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:51.020
or that the three
evaluated points coincide.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.340
We have not yet forced
R and W to equal one.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.070
The separate sign condition
in Node six point three,

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.260
followed by rigidity in Node six point
four, will supply the remaining argument.
